# WordPress Plugin Standards

Applies on top of the core WordPress + PHP standards. Prefer OOP, modular, extensible plugins.

## Structure & bootstrap
- Directory + main file: lowercase-with-hyphens (e.g. `my-plugin/my-plugin.php`) with a plugin header.
- Guard direct access: `defined( 'ABSPATH' ) || exit;`.
- Namespace all classes (e.g. `MyVendor\MyPlugin`) or prefix every function/hook/option to avoid collisions.
- Autoload with Composer PSR-4 for non-trivial plugins; otherwise a small manual loader. Bootstrap via a single entry class.

## Plugin API & lifecycle
- Register behavior through hooks in the bootstrap, not at file scope side effects.
- Lifecycle: `register_activation_hook()` (create tables, default options, flush rewrites), `register_deactivation_hook()` (unschedule cron), and an `uninstall.php` for cleanup.

```php
// my-plugin/my-plugin.php
declare(strict_types=1);
defined( 'ABSPATH' ) || exit;

register_activation_hook( __FILE__, [ \MyVendor\MyPlugin\Installer::class, 'activate' ] );
add_action( 'plugins_loaded', [ \MyVendor\MyPlugin\Plugin::class, 'boot' ] );
```

## Database & options
- Create/upgrade tables with `dbDelta()`; store a schema version in an option and run migrations when it changes.
- Use the Options API for settings; wrap multi-step writes in transactions when integrity matters.

## AJAX & REST
- REST first: `register_rest_route()` with a real `permission_callback` (never `__return_true` for privileged routes).
- Legacy AJAX: hook `wp_ajax_{action}` (and `wp_ajax_nopriv_{action}` only when truly public); verify nonce + capability.

```php
register_rest_route( 'my-plugin/v1', '/items', [
    'methods'             => 'POST',
    'callback'            => [ $controller, 'create_item' ],
    'permission_callback' => static fn (): bool => current_user_can( 'edit_posts' ),
] );
```

## Access, i18n & tooling
- Gate features with roles/capabilities (`current_user_can`), add custom caps on activation when needed.
- i18n: load the text domain (`load_plugin_textdomain`) and wrap strings with the plugin domain.
- Tooling: manage deps with Composer, lint with PHPCS + WordPress-Coding-Standards, write tests with `WP_UnitTestCase`.
